---
source: crates/splot/src/model/validator/types.rs
expression: "messages.join(\"\\n\")"
---
nodes.Home.meshIp: firewall left out - a linux node names the program behind every concern, or none to leave it alone
nodes.Home.meshIp: splot can't manage wireguard with wg-quick on openwrt - there it's netifd, or none
nodes.Home.meshIp: the node has a hub, but splot leaves its WireGuard alone - hub clients are peers on splot's mesh interface; a WireGuard server of your own, with its own subnet, is a zone
nodes.Home.meshIp: fw4 can't name a firewall zone '1lan' - its zone names don't start with a digit, and on OpenWrt this is the zone's name there
nodes.Home.meshIp: an OpenWrt node's zones are its fw4 zones of the same name - interfaces are for nftables, on Linux
nodes.Home.meshIp: 'lab' is neither one of the node's zones nor its wanZone
nodes.Home.meshIp: no interface for zone 'iot' - nftables tells the node's zones apart by their interfaces, so each one, and the wanZone, needs its own
nodes.Home.meshIp: zones iot, lan share eth0 - splot couldn't tell them apart, so each zone needs an interface of its own
nodes.Home.meshIp: spl_mesh is splot's mesh interface, and no zone is on it
nodes.Home.meshIp: name 'Home' is already used as another global name (node, client, or tag)
nodes.Home.meshIp: local name Cabin conflicts with global Cabin - rename one to avoid ambiguity
nodes.Home.meshIp: name 'lan' is already used in the same node
nodes.Home.meshIp: name 'Home_x' uses reserved prefix 'Home_'
nodes.Home.meshIp: tag 'Home' collides with a name used elsewhere in the global namespace
nodes.Home.meshIp: unknown identifier 'Home.nas'
nodes.Home.meshIp: that's the node's own address on the zone - leave gateway out where the node is the zone's gateway
nodes.Home.meshIp: no interface for zone 'lan' - its gateway is another box, and dnsmasq hands out the routes through the node (DHCP option 121) on the zone's interfaces
nodes.Home.meshIp: IP 10.0.0.1 is outside subnet 10.0.0.0/24
nodes.Home.meshIp: IP 10.0.0.1 is the network address of 10.0.0.0/24 and can't be assigned to a host
nodes.Home.meshIp: subnet 10.0.0.0/24 overlaps with another subnet 10.1.0.0/16
nodes.Home.meshIp: hub network 10.0.0.0/24 must lie inside meshNetwork 10.1.0.0/16
nodes.Home.meshIp: mesh IP 10.0.0.1 lies inside the hub network at nodes.Cabin.meshIp - a hub network is routed to its node as a whole, so it can't hold another peer's address
nodes.Home.meshIp: IP 10.0.0.1 is already used at nodes.Cabin.meshIp
nodes.Home.meshIp: client has IP on zone 'iot', but already has an IP on another zone at nodes.Cabin.meshIp (max one zone per (client, node))
nodes.Home.meshIp: no macs - splot's DHCP on Home reserves the address by MAC, so add them (for a host with a static address too: the reservation keeps the pool off it)
nodes.Home.meshIp: splot's DHCP on Home hands out 'my_printer' as a host name, which it can't be: invalid character '_' in "my_printer" (only letters, digits and hyphens) - rename it
nodes.Home.meshIp: 10.0.0.1 isn't on the node - the others forward its domain there over the mesh, so it's in one of its zones, or its meshIp
nodes.Home.meshIp: cabin.home.example.org overlaps Home's domain home.example.org - each node answers a domain of its own, and the others forward it there
nodes.Home.meshIp: splot's DNS on Home names 'my_printer' in its domain, which it can't: invalid character '_' in "my_printer" (only letters, digits and hyphens) - rename it
nodes.Home.meshIp: printer.home.example.org is a name in its domain already, at nodes.Cabin.meshIp (DNS ignores case)
nodes.Home.meshIp: only a service of a device or a hub client gets names, in its node's domain
nodes.Home.meshIp: cabin.example.org is Cabin's domain itself - splot sends an endpoint name to public DNS, and the domain to Cabin: dial a name inside it (wg.cabin.example.org)
nodes.Home.meshIp: Home has no domain to name it in - give it one (`dns: { domain, address }`)
nodes.Home.meshIp: public key required (client has a mesh IP)
nodes.Home.meshIp: node 'Lake' does not exist
nodes.Home.meshIp: public key is already used at nodes.Cabin.meshIp - every node and client needs its own key
nodes.Home.meshIp: node 'Home' has no network named 'iot'
nodes: no node has an endpoint - at least one must, or no node can dial another and the mesh never forms
nodes.Home.meshIp: the node has a hub but no endpoint - hub clients dial their hub node, so it needs one
nodes.Home.meshIp: port 22 collides with nodes.Cabin.meshIp
nodes.Home.meshIp: unknown node 'Lake'
nodes.Home.meshIp: node 'Home' does not have wanZone defined
nodes.Home.meshIp: qualified wan target form ({Node}.{Network}) is only allowed on services hosted by a global client — node/device services have a single unambiguous target and don't need disambiguation
nodes.Home.meshIp: qualified wan target references network 'iot' on node 'Home', but no such zone exists on that node
nodes.Home.meshIp: qualified wan target 'Home.iot' requires this client to have an IP at ips.Home.iot — none declared
nodes.Home.meshIp: client has no reachable address on node 'Home' (no meshIp, no zone IP on this node). Either declare an IP for this client on the node, or use a different wan.via target
nodes.Home.meshIp: wanZone 'lan' collides with another zone at nodes.Cabin.meshIp — wanZone must not match any zone on the same node
nodes.Home.meshIp: others dial node 'Home' here, so splot lets its listen port in from the WAN, but it does not have wanZone defined
